    plt[`:xticks][til count distrib_b;key distrib_b;`rotation pykw `45];
    plt[`:ylabel][`$"#Tweets"];
    plt[`:show][];

![Figure 1](imgs/binary_dist.png)

We can see from this that the dataset contains significantly more of the `affected_individuals` class. Given the dataset being used, this is unsurprising as every effort has been made to make the dataset as relevant as possible by the collators of this dataset. Looking at the multi-class example, we can see how the dataset as a whole breaks down into categories. The code to achieve this is similar to that above and thus not displayed again. ![Figure 2](imgs/multi_dist.png)

As with the binary case, there are a number of classes that are more prominent within the data, such as affected individuals and donations/volunteering. Some classes are seen less often, such as sympathy and prayers.

As such it may be expected that the models produced will be more likely to correctly classify tweets surrounding donations than those relating to prayers. ### Word cloud¶

Similar to the data-distribution case, it is possible to gain some insights into the content of the dataset by looking at commonly occurring words within the classes. This was achieved here through the use of the wordcloud library in Python. The code to achieve this was wrapped in the function `wordcloud`, which functionally is as follows


